# Summary of the Bill by Section

## Subtitle A—Nutrition

**Sec. 10101. Re-evaluation of thrifty food plan.**
This section rewrites the definition of the SNAP "thrifty food plan" to lock in the market baskets from USDA's 2021 report, allowing re-evaluation no earlier than October 1, 2027, and only if cost-neutral (the Secretary may not increase the plan's cost). It codifies household adjustment ratios (e.g., 30% for a 1-person household, 100% for 4-person, capped at 200% for 9+ persons) and requires annual cost-of-living adjustments each October 1 using the CPI for All Urban Consumers for the 12 months ending in June. Conforming amendments update cross-references in sections 16, 19, and 27 of the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008.

**Sec. 10102. Modifications to SNAP work requirements for able-bodied adults.**
This section raises the age range for able-bodied adults without dependents (ABAWDs) subject to the 3-month time limit to 18 through 65 (previously 18 through 54) and narrows the dependent-child exemption to households with a child under 14 (previously under 18). It retains exemptions for medically unfit individuals, pregnant women, and Indians/Urban Indians under the Indian Health Care Improvement Act. It standardizes the unemployment-rate waiver so only "noncontiguous States" (Alaska and Hawaii, excluding Guam and the Virgin Islands) may qualify at 1.5 times the national unemployment rate, and creates a new exemption process for noncontiguous States expiring December 31, 2028, with quarterly reporting requirements.

**Sec. 10103. Availability of standard utility allowances based on receipt of energy assistance.**
This section restricts the standard utility allowance (SUA) so that households receiving Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAA) payments of $20 or more can only use the SUA if the household includes an elderly or disabled member. Households without an elderly or disabled member are limited to the actual utility expenses they incur. This narrows who can claim the higher SUA deduction based on energy assistance receipt.

**Sec. 10104. Restrictions on internet expenses.**
This section adds a new provision to section 5(e)(6) of the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 stating that any service fee associated with internet connection shall not be used in computing the excess shelter expense deduction. This means SNAP households can no longer count internet costs toward their shelter deduction, which reduces their deductible expenses and potentially their benefit amount.

**Sec. 10105. Matching funds requirements.**
This section creates a State cost-sharing requirement for SNAP allotment costs beginning in fiscal year 2028, based on each State's payment error rate: error rates below 6% get 100% federal funding, 6–8% get 95% federal/5% State, 8–10% get 90% federal/10% State, and 10% or above get 85% federal/15% State. States may elect to use their FY2025 or FY2026 error rate for the FY2028 calculation, and thereafter the error rate from three fiscal years prior applies. States with high error rates (multiplied by 1.5 equal to or above 20%) in FY2025 or FY2026 get delayed implementation to FY2029 or FY2030, respectively.

**Sec. 10106. Administrative cost sharing.**
This section reduces the federal reimbursement of State SNAP administrative costs from 50 percent to 25 percent, effective for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter (50 percent continues through fiscal year 2026). This shifts half of administrative costs to the States.

**Sec. 10107. National education and obesity prevention grant program.**
This section amends section 28(d)(1)(F) of the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to change the funding authorization for the SNAP-Ed program (National Education and Obesity Prevention Grant Program) from "fiscal year 2016 and each subsequent fiscal year" to only "each of fiscal years 2016 through 2025." This effectively ends the permanent authorization, requiring new legislation to continue funding beyond FY2025.

**Sec. 10108. Alien SNAP eligibility.**
This section rewrites section 6(f) of the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to restrict SNAP eligibility to U.S. residents who are (1) U.S. citizens or nationals, (2) lawfully admitted permanent resident immigrants, (3) Cuban and Haitian entrants under the Refugee Education Assistance Act of 1980, or (4) individuals lawfully residing under a Compact of Free Association. It excludes visitors, tourists, diplomats, and students entering temporarily. The income and resources of an ineligible household member still count toward the household's eligibility and allotment determination (with a State-option pro rata share).

## Subtitle B—Forestry

**Sec. 10201. Rescission of amounts for forestry.**
This section rescinds unobligated balances appropriated by the Inflation Reduction Act (Public Law 117-169) for forestry programs, specifically paragraphs (3) and (4) of section 23001(a), paragraphs (1) through (4) of section 23002(a), section 23003(a)(2), and section 23005. These provisions funded State and private forestry conservation, including urban and community forestry and landscape-scale restoration.

## Subtitle C—Commodities

**Sec. 10301. Effective reference price; reference price.**
This section raises the effective reference price calculation from 85 percent to 88 percent (beginning with crop year 2025) of the Olympic average of marketing year prices, and sets new statutory reference prices effective for the 2025 crop year (e.g., wheat $6.35/bushel, corn $4.10/bushel, soybeans $10.00/bushel, seed cotton $0.42/pound, peanuts $630.00/ton). Beginning with the 2031 crop year, reference prices increase by a factor of 1.005 annually, capped at 113 percent of the 2025 statutory levels.

**Sec. 10302. Base acres.**
This section extends the base acre update deadline from 2023 to 2031 and authorizes the Secretary to allocate up to 30,000,000 additional base acres nationwide to eligible farms, based on planting history from the 2019–2023 crop years (including prevented planting) plus up to 15 percent of total farm acres planted to noncovered commodities. Farms with no covered commodity plantings in that period are ineligible, and owners may decline the allocation within 90 days of notice. If total eligible acres exceed 30 million, the Secretary applies an across-the-board pro-rata reduction, and payment yields for new base acres are set using existing farm yields or county averages.

**Sec. 10303. Producer election.**
This section extends the Agriculture Risk Coverage (ARC) and Price Loss Coverage (PLC) election period through the 2031 crop year and adds a one-time election opportunity for the 2026 crop year, with the 2027–2031 elections locked to match the 2025 crop year coverage. It also adds a new subsection (i) requiring the Secretary, for the 2025 crop year only, to pay producers the higher of PLC payments or ARC county coverage payments on a covered commodity-by-commodity basis. A conforming amendment to the Federal Crop Insurance Act makes all acres eligible for the Supplemental Coverage Option regardless of ARC/PLC election.

**Sec. 10304. Price loss coverage.**
This section extends the Price Loss Coverage (PLC) program through the 2031 crop year (previously 2023) and updates the payment yield calculation period from 2012–2016 to 2017–2021. It also updates a cross-reference to the reference price definition in section 1111(19)(A)(vi) of the Agricultural Act of 2014.

**Sec. 10305. Agriculture risk coverage.**
This section extends the Agriculture Risk Coverage (ARC) program through the 2031 crop year and increases the guarantee band: for 2025–2031 crop years, the benchmark revenue guarantee rises to 90 percent (from 86 percent) and the payment trigger threshold drops to 12 percent of benchmark revenue (from 10 percent), while 2014–2024 crop years retain the prior 10 percent trigger. The effective reference price calculation within ARC is also updated to 88 percent consistent with Sec. 10301.

**Sec. 10306. Equitable treatment of certain entities.**
This section defines a new term, "qualified pass-through entity," to include partnerships, S corporations, limited liability companies not electing corporate treatment, and joint ventures or general partnerships under the Food Security Act of 1985. It replaces the prior references to "joint ventures and general partnerships" throughout sections 1001, 1001A, and 1001B, extending payment attribution, actively-engaged-in-farming rules, and joint and several liability provisions to these entities uniformly. It also removes a limitation that previously tied these rules only to subtitle B of title I of the Agricultural Act of 2014.

**Sec. 10307. Payment limitations.**
This section raises the payment limitation for PLC and ARC payments from $125,000 to $155,000 per person or legal entity (subject to a new subsection (i)), and subjects both the $155,000 general limitation and the $155,000 limitation for certain entities to an annual inflation adjustment. Beginning with the 2025 crop year, the Secretary shall adjust these amounts annually based on the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics.

**Sec. 10308. Adjusted gross income limitation.**
This section adds an exception to the adjusted gross income (AGI) limitation (currently $900,000 for commodity and conservation payments) for persons or legal entities deriving 75 percent or more of their average gross income from farming, ranching, or silviculture activities. The exception applies to "excepted payments or benefits," defined as payments under subtitle E of title I of the Agricultural Act of 2014 (livestock disaster programs), section 196 of the 1996 farm bill, and certain payments described in paragraph (2)(C) received on or after October 1, 2024. Farming activities expressly include agri-tourism, direct-to-consumer marketing, and sale of agricultural equipment.

**Sec. 10309. Marketing loans.**
This section extends marketing assistance loan availability through the 2031 crop year and sets new loan rates for 2026–2031 crop years (e.g., wheat $3.72/bushel, corn $2.42/bushel, upland cotton $0.55/pound, soybeans $6.82/bushel, rice $7.70/hundredweight, peanuts $390/ton), while keeping 2025 rates unchanged. It increases the extra long staple cotton loan rate premium from $0.25 to $0.30 and establishes cotton storage payments for 2026–2031 crop years at the lesser of the submitted storage charge or $4.90 (California/Arizona) or $3.00 (other States). It also extends loan deficiency payments, payments in lieu of loan deficiency payments for grazed acreage, recourse loans, and extra long staple cotton competitive provisions through 2031–2032.

**Sec. 10310. Repayment of marketing loans.**
This section restructures marketing loan repayment rates so that rice and upland cotton repay at the prevailing world market price (rather than the loan rate), with upland cotton producers receiving a refund equal to the difference between the lowest prevailing world price in the 30 days after repayment and the repayment rate. It adds extra long staple cotton to the world price formula (based on the average of the 3 lowest-priced growths when Middling 1-3/32-inch quotations are available) and allows the Secretary to adjust the extra long staple cotton world price through July 31, 2032, to minimize forfeitures, government stock accumulation, and ensure competitive marketing.

**Sec. 10311. Economic adjustment assistance for textile mills.**
This section increases the economic adjustment assistance payment rate for domestic textile mills from 3 cents per pound to 5 cents per pound, effective beginning August 1, 2025. The 3 cents per pound rate applies retroactively for the period August 1, 2013, through July 31, 2025.

**Sec. 10312. Sugar program updates.**
This section raises the raw cane sugar loan rate to 24.00 cents per pound for 2025–2031 crop years (from 18.75 cents) and sets the refined beet sugar loan rate at 136.55 percent of the raw cane rate, while increasing CCC storage rates for forfeited sugar to 34 cents per hundredweight per month for refined sugar and 27 cents for raw cane sugar. It modernizes beet sugar allotment reallocation by prioritizing processors with available sugar and requiring initial reassignment within 30 days of the January World Agricultural Supply and Demand Estimates release. It also directs the Secretary to reallocate tariff-rate quota shortfalls among importing countries (ceasing if the 2014 U.S.–Mexico sugar suspension agreement terminates), requires a study within 180 days on additional terms for refined sugar imports (with a report to Congress within 1 year), and extends the sugar program through the 2031 crop year.

**Sec. 10313. Dairy policy updates.**
This section changes the Dairy Margin Coverage (DMC) production history calculation to the highest annual milk marketings from any one of the 2021, 2022, or 2023 calendar years, with new dairy operations electing either extrapolated actual marketings or a herd-size-based estimate. It raises the Tier I coverage cap from 5,000,000 to 6,000,000 pounds of milk production history and extends the DMC program through the 2031 calendar year. Premium discounts for Tier I coverage are extended for 2026–2031, with the discount calculation updated to use January 2026 as the baseline.

**Sec. 10314. Implementation.**
This section provides $50,000,000 in Commodity Credit Corporation funds (available until expended) to implement subtitle C, including $5,000,000 for base acre and producer election activities, $3,000,000 each for two categories of producer outreach, $9,000,000 for mandatory biennial surveys of dairy production costs and product yields reported by manufacturers under section 273 of the Agricultural Marketing Act of 1946, and $1,000,000 for the refined sugar import study under Sec. 10312.

## Subtitle D—Disaster Assistance Programs

**Sec. 10401. Supplemental agricultural disaster assistance.**
This section increases Livestock Indemnity Program (LIP) payment rates to 100 percent of market value for losses due to predation and 75 percent for losses due to adverse weather or disease, and adds new payments for unborn livestock losses (incurred on or after January 1, 2024) at up to 85 percent of the lowest weight class rate, with multipliers by species (e.g., 12 for swine, average litter size for other species). It expands the Livestock Forage Program (LFP) to provide 2 monthly payments for severe drought and eligibility after 4 consecutive weeks of extreme drought during the normal grazing period. It also adds new Emergency Assistance (ELAP) payments for farm-raised fish losses from piscivorous birds at not less than $600 per acre (paid on 85 percent of acres), sets a 15 percent normal mortality rate for honeybee colony losses, and increases the Tree Assistance Program (TAP) replacement threshold from 50 percent to 65 percent mortality while removing the 15 percent mortality/damage triggers in favor of normal mortality standards.

## Subtitle E—Crop Insurance

**Sec. 10501. Beginning farmer and rancher benefit.**
This section extends the definition of a "beginning farmer or rancher" from 5 to 10 reinsurance years of farming/ranching experience and adds additional premium support on top of existing benefits: 10 percentage points in years 1–2, 5 percentage points in years 3–5, and 5 percentage points in years 6–10 of participation. A conforming amendment strikes an obsolete subparagraph in section 522(c)(7).

**Sec. 10502. Area-based crop insurance coverage and affordability.**
This section raises the maximum coverage level for area yield or revenue coverage plans to 95 percent (from 90 percent) and for individual yield or revenue coverage aggregated across multiple commodities to 90 percent (from 85 percent), while individual coverage remains at 85 percent. It increases the premium subsidy for area plans at the 90 percent coverage level from 51 percent to 62 percent and adjusts related subsidy tiers (e.g., the 86 percent level becomes 90 percent with subsidy rising from 55 to 68 percent).

**Sec. 10503. Administrative and operating expense adjustments.**
This section directs the Risk Management Agency's Corporation to pay approved insurance providers an additional administrative and operating (A&O) expense subsidy equal to 6 percent of net book premium for eligible contracts in "eligible States" (where loss ratios exceed 120 percent of total net book premium), beginning with the 2026 reinsurance year, excluding catastrophic, area-based, and no-loss-adjustment policies. It sets a minimum A&O reimbursement for specialty crop contracts at the greater of 17 percent of premium or the Standard Reinsurance Agreement rate, and provides an inflation adjustment to total A&O reimbursements beginning in 2026, capped for that year at the prior year's CPI-U change.

**Sec. 10504. Premium support.**
This section increases federal premium subsidies for crop insurance by 5 percentage points at each coverage level: 69 percent (from 64) at the 50–60 percent coverage level, 64 percent (from 59) at 65 percent, 60 percent (from 55) at 70 percent, 51 percent (from 48) at 75 percent, and 41 percent (from 38) at 80 percent and above.

**Sec. 10505. Program compliance and integrity.**
This section raises the annual funding cap for the Office of Inspector General's crop insurance compliance and integrity reviews from $4,000,000 (fiscal years 2009–2025) to $6,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each subsequent fiscal year.

**Sec. 10506. Reviews, compliance, and integrity.**
This section increases the annual funding for the Risk Management Agency's information technology and data mining reviews from the prior baseline to $10,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each fiscal year thereafter (fiscal years 2014–2025 retain the prior amount).

**Sec. 10507. Poultry insurance pilot program.**
This section directs the Federal Crop Insurance Corporation to establish a pilot program offering index-based insurance to contract poultry growers (including broiler and laying hen growers) against extreme weather-related risks that increase utility costs (natural gas, propane, electricity, water, and other costs determined by the Corporation). The pilot must be conducted in a sufficient number of counties in top poultry-producing States to evaluate feasibility, effectiveness, and producer demand, with stakeholder engagement required and Board approval of a policy or plan due not later than 2 years after enactment.

## Subtitle F—Additional Investments in Rural America

**Sec. 10601. Conservation.**
This section sets mandatory funding for conservation programs for fiscal years 2026–2031, including the Environmental Quality Incentives Program ($625 million rising to $700 million annually), Conservation Stewardship Program ($2.655 billion rising to $3.255 billion annually), Agricultural Conservation Easement Program ($1.3 billion rising to $1.375 billion annually), and the Regional Conservation Partnership Program ($425 million rising to $450 million annually). It also extends the Grassroots Source Water Protection Program ($1 million beginning FY2026), Voluntary Public Access and Habitat Incentive Program ($70 million for FY2025–2031), Watershed Protection and Flood Prevention operations ($150 million annually for FY2026 and thereafter, up from $50 million), and the Feral Swine Eradication and Control Pilot Program ($105 million for FY2025–2031). It rescinds unobligated Inflation Reduction Act (Public Law 117-169) conservation funds from section 21001(a).

**Sec. 10602. Supplemental agricultural trade promotion program.**
This section directs the Secretary of Agriculture to carry out a program to encourage the accessibility, development, maintenance, and expansion of commercial export markets for U.S. agricultural commodities, funded at $285,000,000 from Commodity Credit Corporation funds for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter.

**Sec. 10603. Nutrition.**
This section extends the funding authorization for section 203D(d)(5) of the Emergency Food Assistance Act of 1983 (The Emergency Food Assistance Program, TEFAP) from fiscal year 2024 through fiscal year 2031.

**Sec. 10604. Research.**
This section provides mandatory funding for several research programs: $37 million to the Foundation for Food and Agriculture Research (within 30 days of enactment), $60 million for scholarships at 1890 Institutions (historically Black land-grant universities) for FY2026, $8 million for the Assistive Technology Program for Farmers With Disabilities for FY2026, $175 million for the Specialty Crop Research Initiative for FY2026 (up from $80 million annually), and $125 million annually for the Research Facilities Act competitive grant program for FY2026 and thereafter. It also extends the Urban, Indoor, and Other Emerging Agricultural Production Research, Education, and Extension Initiative through fiscal year 2031.

**Sec. 10605. Energy.**
This section extends the mandatory funding authorization for the Rural Energy for America Program (REAP) under section 9005(g)(1)(F) of the Farm Security and Rural Investment Act of 2002 from fiscal year 2024 through fiscal year 2031.

**Sec. 10606. Horticulture.**
This section increases and extends funding for horticulture programs: Plant Pest and Disease Management rises to $90 million for FY2026 (from $75 million), Specialty Crop Block Grants rise to $100 million for FY2026 (from $85 million), the Organic Production and Market Data Initiative receives $10 million for FY2026–2031, international trade technology systems receive $5 million for FY2026 (up from $1 million), and the National Organic Certification Cost-Share Program is extended through FY2031. It also adds $5 million for FY2026 for the Multiple Crop and Pesticide Use Survey.

**Sec. 10607. Miscellaneous.**
This section provides $233 million annually for fiscal years 2026–2030 for animal disease prevention and management (with at least $10 million for subsection (a), $70 million for subsection (b), and $153 million for subsection (c)), dropping to $75 million for FY2031 and thereafter (with at least $45 million for subsection (b)). It also adds $3 million for FY2026 to the Sheep Production and Marketing Grant Program and extends the Pima Agriculture Cotton Trust Fund, Agriculture Wool Apparel Manufacturers Trust Fund, Wool Research and Promotion, and Emergency Citrus Disease Research and Development Trust Fund through fiscal year 2031 (from 2024). [end of text]


